Decomposition [dB]; positive = SNR lost
model     activity  tilt effect  pose-blind  outage +
cosine    sitting         -5.75        0.00      2.5%
cosine    walking         -4.92        0.00      1.7%
specular  sitting         -5.21        2.58      3.4%
specular  walking         -4.34        2.24      2.3%

  tilt effect = face-up -> genie. NEGATIVE means tilting HELPS: the
                IRS panels sit on the walls at z = 1.5 m, so a face-up
                PD is badly aimed for wall reflections. Mean SNR rises,
                but see the outage column -- the spread widens sharply.
  pose-blind  = genie -> blind, the cost of the controller not knowing
                the device pose, i.e. what assuming a face-up PD spends.

  NOTE the cosine-model rows: pose-blind is exactly 0.00 dB. This is
  structural, not a rounding artefact. Under the base paper's model the
  optimal normal is n = u_out, which depends only on mirror and receiver
  POSITIONS. Device orientation cannot change the optimal steering, so
  knowing the pose is worth precisely nothing. Under specular reflection
  the optimum is the bisector, the LED direction enters, and pose
  knowledge is worth real dB. Orientation-aware IRS control is therefore
  unmodellable in the prevailing channel model -- a second consequence
  of omitting the law of reflection.

