======================================================================
WHEN IS PREDICTION WORTH IT?
======================================================================

Pointing footprint at 3 m: 5.2 cm. Aim error of that order
costs the whole IRS gain, whichever way the error arises.

  reactive closed form : aim point is stale by v * tau_slew
  predictive           : aim point carries the tracking error sigma

So prediction only pays when the staleness it removes exceeds the
estimation error it introduces:

      v * tau_slew  >  sigma

  slew  10 ms at 1.0 m/s -> staleness   1.0 cm; predict only if sigma < that
  slew  20 ms at 1.0 m/s -> staleness   2.0 cm; predict only if sigma < that
  slew  50 ms at 1.0 m/s -> staleness   5.0 cm; predict only if sigma < that

Measured: which scheme wins (mean SNR, dB)
                sigma=0.00    sigma=0.05    sigma=0.10    sigma=0.20
  slew  10 ms  predict  4.2 reactive  9.4 reactive 10.7 reactive 11.1
  slew  20 ms  predict  9.6 reactive  3.9 reactive  5.2 reactive  5.7
  slew  50 ms  predict 13.5  predict  0.3 reactive  0.9 reactive  1.4

  Sub-centimetre tracking is not available from VLC positioning, so
  the ROBUST recommendation is the reactive closed form: it recovers
  73% of the bound at 10 ms slew with no pose estimate at all, and its
  performance does not depend on tracking accuracy. Prediction is an
  upgrade for systems that can localise to a few centimetres, not the
  headline contribution.

